[38;5;231m⏺[39m One last question before I present the design. This repo has no test script, no linter, and no dependencies — and the
  pieces you've chosen (denylist redaction, ring-buffer eviction) are exactly the kind of logic that fails silently and
  only shows up when you need the logs most. Cheapest moment to set tooling up is now, before the code exists.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what infrastructure the logger ships with.

  - [1mUnit tests via [38;5;153mnode:test[0m — built into Node, zero dependencies, [38;5;153mnode --test[39m as the [38;5;153mtest[39m script. Buys you real
    coverage of redaction and buffer eviction. Costs one test file. The browser-facing behavior is testable because the
    logger is dual-mode — Node can import it directly.
  - [1mLint + format[0m — ESLint + Prettier. Buys consistency, costs two dev dependencies and config in a repo that currently
    has neither. Lower value at four files than it'd be at forty.
  - [1mNeither[0m — ship the logger alone. Fastest; the redaction list has no regression net.

  [1mMy recommendation: unit tests only.[0m Zero dependencies and it covers the logic that actually carries risk. Linting can
  come later without rework.

